---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StockCountLab from "../../components/StockCountLab.tsx";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Many Stocks Is Enough?"
  description="Diversifying within stocks reduces risk — but only down to a floor. See the classic risk-vs-number-of-stocks curve and the systematic risk it can't remove."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>How many stocks is enough?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Diversification isn't just about mixing asset classes — it works
      <em>within</em> stocks too. Hold more of them and your portfolio steadies,
      because each company's own quirks average out. But there's a catch: because
      stocks tend to move together, you can only push risk down to a floor, never
      to zero.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <StockCountLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The math, in one line</h2>
      <p>
        For <strong>N</strong> equally-weighted stocks, each with volatility
        <strong>σ</strong> and sharing an average correlation <strong>ρ</strong>,
        the portfolio's volatility is:
      </p>
      <p style="text-align:center;font-size:var(--step-1)">
        σ<sub>portfolio</sub> = σ · √( ρ + (1 − ρ) / N )
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The <strong>(1 − ρ)/N</strong> piece is company-specific risk. It
          shrinks toward zero as you add stocks — this is what diversification
          removes, for free.
        </li>
        <li>
          The <strong>ρ</strong> piece never leaves. As N grows, volatility bottoms
          out at <strong>σ√ρ</strong> — the systematic, market-wide risk.
        </li>
        <li>
          In variance terms you've removed <strong>(N − 1)/N</strong> of the
          diversifiable risk — 90% by 10 stocks, 95% by 20. Hence the old rule of
          thumb that a few dozen stocks gets you most of the way.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That irreducible floor is the bridge to the next idea: it's exactly the
        systematic risk that the <a href="/tools/capm">Capital Asset Pricing
        Model</a> says you're paid for — measured by beta. Company-specific risk,
        by contrast, is unpaid, because anyone can diversify it away.
      </p>
      <p>Educational only, not advice.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
